Ports patches are formatted with plain git format-patch in a ports
tree, so the check in format-patch.sh never sees them, and the signoz
port went through five revisions without an interdiff. The skill now
shows how to rebuild the previous version from the list onto the new
base and pass its head to --interdiff, with a range-diff when it no
longer applies.
---
change-id: ypsuyxtrzmyzrzouqztspuomyuykuvyv

 .claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/.claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md b/.claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md
index 5b2ce91..7f7f831 100644
--- a/.claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md
+++ b/.claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md
@@ -202,6 +202,20 @@ For more than one patch add `--cover-letter` and fill it in. Below the
 review: the ports you followed, what was checked (lint, makesum), what
 was not (the build, the plist), and any loosened dependency bound.
 
+A revision adds `-v N` and an interdiff against v(N-1) as posted, so
+Pat sees what changed without rereading the port. Rebuild v(N-1) from
+the list onto the new base, from inside the ports tree, and pass its
+head to git:
+
+    prev="$(~/lab/default.jj/libexec/mail/series-rebuild.sh \
+        -o "${base}" "<message-id of v(N-1)>")"
+    git format-patch ... -v N --interdiff="${prev#*..}" origin/main..HEAD
+
+If v(N-1) does not apply onto the new base, run series-rebuild.sh
+without `-o` and pass `--range-diff="${prev}"` instead; an interdiff
+across two bases would show every commit that landed between them.
+End the notes with what changed and a `Link to v(N-1):` line.
+
 Deliver the series into the mail workspace and push it, as
 zib/patch-review-workflow.org describes:
 
